### Task 10: The table is told a deck is on

**Files:**
- Create: `apps/web/src/ui/Toast.tsx`, `apps/web/src/ui/Toast.test.tsx`
- Modify: `apps/web/src/run/Members.tsx` (the Attached panel)

**Interfaces:**
- Consumes: `useAttachedDecks` from Task 6.
- Produces: `useToast(): { show: (text: string) => void; node: ReactNode }`.

- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**

```tsx
it("shows a note and takes it away again", async () => {
  vi.useFakeTimers();
  function Probe() {
    const toast = useToast();
    return (<><button onClick={() => toast.show("Stream Deck on Thursday")}>go</button>{toast.node}</>);
  }
  render(<Probe />);
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByText("go"));
  expect(screen.getByRole("status")).toHaveTextContent("Stream Deck on Thursday");
  await act(() => vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(6000));
  expect(screen.queryByRole("status")).toBeNull();
});
```

- [ ] **Step 2: Run test to verify it fails**

Run: `npx vitest run apps/web/src/ui/Toast.test.tsx`
Expected: FAIL — cannot resolve `./Toast.tsx`.

- [ ] **Step 3: Write the toast**

```tsx
import { useCallback, useEffect, useState, type ReactNode } from "react";

/**
 * A line that says something happened and then goes away.
 *
 * The app has said things in place until now, under the thing that did
 * them. A deck attaching has no place of its own: the press came from
 * across the room, and the person who made it is looking at the game.
 * Five seconds, `role="status"` so it is read out rather than announced,
 * and no buttons: nothing here is worth interrupting for.
 */
export function useToast(): { show: (text: string) => void; node: ReactNode } {
  const [text, setText] = useState<string | null>(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    if (text === null) return;
    const timer = window.setTimeout(() => setText(null), 5000);
    return () => window.clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [text]);
  const show = useCallback((next: string) => setText(next), []);
  const node = text === null ? null : (
    <div className="toast" role="status">{text}</div>
  );
  return { show, node };
}
```

Add a `.toast` rule to `apps/web/src/styles.css` using the existing surface and border tokens; keep it bottom-center and out of the run's own column.

- [ ] **Step 4: Say it when a deck arrives**

In `RunView.tsx`, watch the count from `useAttachedDecks` and show the note when it rises:

```tsx
const toast = useToast();
const hadDecks = useRef(0);
useEffect(() => {
  if (decks > hadDecks.current) toast.show(decks === 1 ? "A Stream Deck is on this run." : `${decks} Stream Decks are on this run.`);
  hadDecks.current = decks;
}, [decks, toast]);
```

Render `{toast.node}` beside the existing panels. In `Members.tsx`, add the deck to the Attached list so the table can see it at rest as well as when it arrives — the copy is "Stream Deck", with no address beside it, since a deck signs in rather than taking a line to paste.

- [ ] **Step 5: Run the tests**

Run: `npx vitest run apps/web/src && npm run typecheck`
Expected: PASS.

- [ ] **Step 6: Commit**

```bash
git add apps/web/src/ui/Toast.tsx apps/web/src/ui/Toast.test.tsx apps/web/src/styles.css apps/web/src/run/RunView.tsx apps/web/src/run/Members.tsx
git commit -m "feat(run): the table is told when a deck is on the run"
```

